# CouchUI — Version Log

Newest first. Entries cover user-facing changes, notable fixes, and
anything that changes where files live or how things are configured.

Versions before 0.1.0 are reconstructed retrospectively from the change
history — they were not tagged at the time.

Patch releases (0.8.1, 0.8.2, …) are the default, including for new
features. The minor version is reserved for substantial milestones —
a reworked subsystem, a breaking config change, or anything needing a
migration step.

---

## 0.8.2

* Added rotation to `error.log`: at 1 MB it rolls to `error.log.1`, keeping
  one generation, so disk use is bounded at 2 MB. Without this a failure
  that repeats every few seconds — like the Kodi AppArmor denial loop —
  would grow the file indefinitely on a machine left running for months.
* Changed the Kodi tile to the Flatpak build (`flatpak run tv.kodi.Kodi`).
  The snap build's confinement blocked external drives, which is why it
  couldn't read media from `/run/media`. `install.sh --apps` already
  installed the Flatpak version and granted it filesystem access; the tile
  now points at it.
* Changed the Moonlight tile to its Flatpak build for the same consistency
  with what the installer provides.

## 0.8.1

* Changed the versioning convention: patch bumps are now the default for
  ordinary work, including new features, with the minor version reserved
  for substantial milestones or anything needing a migration step.
  Releases 0.1 through 0.8 landed in quick succession, which made the
  minor number carry less meaning than it should.

## 0.8.0

* Rewrote `install.sh` as a bulk installer with composable flags:
  `--deps`, `--apps`, `--firewall`, `--autostart`, `--shortcuts`, `--all`.
  Every step is idempotent, so re-running is safe.
* Added `--apps`, which installs the applications the tiles point at:
  Brave (official apt repo), Kodi and Moonlight (Flatpak), Spotify
  (official snap), plus KDE Connect, AntiMicroX, onboard and cec-utils.
  Kodi is granted Flatpak access to `/run/media`, `/media` and home —
  the snap build's confinement was what blocked external drives.
* Added `--firewall`, opening TCP and UDP 1714-1764 for KDE Connect.
* Changed the Electron sandbox fix to locate `chrome-sandbox` by searching
  `node_modules` rather than assuming a fixed path, and to skip when the
  permissions are already correct. It also reports clearly when Electron
  hasn't finished downloading instead of failing.
* Changed every generated file (the `couchui` command, the desktop entry,
  the systemd unit) to use absolute paths derived from the script's own
  location, so moving the project and re-running fixes all of them.
* Removed `setup-shortcuts.sh` — folded into `install.sh --shortcuts` so
  there's one entry point rather than two.
* The installer does not rewrite `config.json`; it prints the commands the
  Flatpak apps need and leaves the tile config alone.

## 0.7.0

* Added a **Phone remote** pane in Settings for KDE Connect pairing, so a
  phone can be used as a trackpad and keyboard — the practical answer to
  navigating web pages a controller can't. Lists devices with their state,
  with Pair / Unpair / Test actions; unpair takes a second press to
  confirm.
* Added handling for KDE Connect not being installed: the pane shows the
  install command and the firewall ports rather than reporting an error,
  since that's a normal first-run state.
* All KDE Connect actions go through `kdeconnect-cli`, which activates the
  daemon itself, so there's no separate service for the launcher to start
  or monitor.

## 0.6.2

* Fixed the progress bar appearing split into two pieces with a gap partway
  along it. The fill animated its `width`, which forces a layout and repaint
  every frame and left a visible unpainted seam on a 3px-tall bar. It now
  animates `transform: scaleX()` instead, which is composited on the GPU
  with no repaint, so there's nothing to tear. Measured from a screenshot:
  the "dead sector" was a 3px band of the track colour showing through
  between the old and new paint regions.
* Added a volume popup to the Now Playing bar (🔊 button, or **V**). It
  appears above the bar and captures input — left/right adjust in 5% steps,
  other buttons are ignored — and dismisses on **B**/Escape or after 10
  seconds without an adjustment, with the timer resetting on each change.
  Held input is debounced to one API call at the final level.

## 0.6.1

* Added a **Connected devices** list at the top of Settings → Connections:
  device name with its MAC address beneath, "Connected" state, and a
  battery icon with percentage on the right, plus a Disconnect button.
  Battery level comes from `bluetoothctl info` per device; peripherals
  that don't report one show "No battery info" rather than a blank.
* Added a **Web shortcuts** pane for adding, editing and removing web
  tiles from the UI rather than hand-editing `config.json`. A bare domain
  gets `https://` added, duplicate names are rejected, and removal needs a
  second press to confirm. Saving only rewrites the `web-menu` submenu and
  preserves everything else in the file, including the Spotify client ID.

## 0.6.0

* Added `"type": "web"` tiles — browser shortcuts that open a single site
  in the browser's app mode (chromeless, no tabs or address bar) so it
  behaves like a dedicated app. Browser flags live once in a top-level
  `web` config block instead of being repeated per tile.
* Added a **Web** submenu to the default config with YouTube (its D-pad TV
  interface), Netflix and Twitch as examples.
* Fixed launching any app from inside a submenu. The tile lookup used a
  flat `find()` over top-level tiles only, so a nested tile failed with
  "Unknown tile" — submenus shipped in 0.4.x but were never exercised with
  a real app tile inside one. The lookup is now recursive.

## 0.5.2

* Fixed navigation stopping partway along a row, where left/right hit a
  phantom "end of row" and up/down had to be used to reach the remaining
  tiles. Row detection compared `offsetTop` for exact equality, so a single
  pixel of difference — from a wrapped label, an uneven tile height, or
  sub-pixel rounding — split one visual row into several logical ones. It
  now groups tiles that overlap vertically (tolerance of half a tile
  height), which is what "on the same row" actually means. Genuine
  multi-row layouts still split correctly.
  The same bug explained focus repeatedly jumping back to the first tile:
  the short phantom rows clamped the column index to 0.
* Fixed the tile row overflowing off the right of the screen, which left
  the last tile clipped and unreachable. Grid items default to
  `min-width: auto`, so a long label ("Android Apps") forced its column
  wider than its share — the classic CSS grid blowout. Tiles now set
  `min-width: 0` and long labels wrap instead of demanding width.

## 0.5.1

* Fixed the launcher continuing to navigate its own tile grid while a
  launched app had focus — pressing a direction in Moonlight moved
  CouchUI's selection underneath it at the same time. The Gamepad API
  reports input regardless of which window is focused, and the poll loop
  wasn't checking. Apps that take focus properly (Kodi) hid this; ones
  that don't, exposed it.
* Changed the controller status line to show when input has been handed to
  a running app, and to clear held-button state so a button still pressed
  when focus returns isn't read as a new press.
* Added custom tile images: a tile can set `image` in `config.json` to any
  local `.png`/`.jpg`/`.svg`/`.webp`, replacing its built-in icon. Paths
  are relative to the app folder (`assets/tiles/...`), keeping
  machine-specific paths out of config; absolute paths also work. A
  missing file degrades to a plain tile and is logged rather than showing
  a broken image.

## 0.5.0

* Added APK installation to the Waydroid hub: an **＋ Install APK** tile
  opens a controller-navigable file browser starting in `~/Downloads`.
  Built as a tile grid rather than the native file dialog, which is
  mouse-and-keyboard only and unusable from a couch.
* Added an APK-only filter as the default, with a toggle tile to show all
  files. Folders are always listed so navigation still works; hidden files
  are excluded either way, and `.APK` matches case-insensitively.
* Changed the Waydroid hub to still render its grid when no apps are
  installed. It previously replaced the whole grid with a message telling
  you to install one from a terminal — which also hid the Install tile, at
  exactly the moment it's most useful.
* Install confirmation comes from re-querying the app list rather than the
  command's exit code, so a listed app is real evidence it worked.

## 0.4.3

* Fixed the white native scrollbar showing down the right of the Spotify
  home screen. The horizontal scrollbars on each row were hidden back in
  0.0.3, but the vertical one on the container holding them never was.
* Changed this to a house rule rather than another one-off: native
  scrollbars are now hidden across the whole launcher via a `*` selector,
  so a new scrolling container can't reintroduce one. The floating
  `.row-scrollbar` is the pattern to use where an indicator is wanted.
* Changed the settings window scrollbars to a slim themed style instead of
  hiding them — it's a normal desktop window used with a mouse, so a
  scrollbar is still useful there; it just shouldn't be the default white.

## 0.4.2

* Added `push.sh` — commits and pushes to your git remote, defaulting the
  commit message to the current version so history lines up with this file.
* Added a safety check to `push.sh` that refuses to push if `config.json`
  has somehow become tracked by git. `.gitignore` only covers untracked
  files, so a config committed before the ignore rule existed would keep
  going out with every push; this catches that and explains how to undo it.

## 0.4.1

* Added `config.example.json` and a `.gitignore` that excludes
  `config.json`, so the Spotify client ID and weather location stay local
  and can't be committed by accident. The app creates `config.json` from
  the example on first run, so a fresh clone still starts.
* Added `.gitignore` entries for `node_modules/`, `error.log`, `dist/` and
  editor noise.

## 0.4.0

* Fixed skip (next/previous) stopping playback instead of changing track.
  Transport commands were sent without a device id, so Spotify applied them
  to "the active device" — and when nothing was active, which is normal for
  a client that just launched or went idle, the call failed and playback
  simply stopped. Every command now names its device explicitly.
* Fixed playback failing when Spotify wasn't already open. A freshly
  launched client registers as a device slightly before it can accept
  playback, and there was no retry, so the first attempt always lost that
  race. Play now retries over a few seconds and transfers playback to the
  device when it isn't the active one.
* Fixed the lag before a track started. The device was rediscovered from
  scratch on every action, costing several sequential API round trips
  before any sound. It's now resolved once, cached, and kept in step with
  whatever is actually playing.
* Changed Premium-required errors (403) to fail immediately with a clear
  message instead of being retried pointlessly.
* Added a shuffle toggle to the Now Playing bar, saved as a preference and
  applied when starting a playlist or album. It's set before playback
  begins so the first track is already shuffled.
* Added queueing — X on a track appends it to the up-next queue rather than
  interrupting the current track — and an **Up Next** row on the Spotify
  home screen showing what's coming.
* Changed X to be contextual: pin on a playlist, queue on a track, toggle
  shuffle anywhere else.

## 0.3.3

* Fixed city search failing with "fetch failed". The lookup was running in
  the main process, which uses Node's network stack rather than Chromium's
  and so doesn't share its proxy/DNS configuration — it can fail even when
  the app reaches the network fine elsewhere. Moved it into the settings
  renderer, the same place the weather lookup already fetches from.

## 0.3.2

* Fixed city search never returning results. API results were being passed
  through a local text filter that expected a pre-built search string;
  since they had none, every result was discarded and the list always read
  "No matches." The filter is now skipped for results a server already
  matched.
* Changed the time zone list from all ~418 IANA zones to a curated ~56
  covering the major population centres and every widely used offset. DST
  still comes from Intl, so behaviour is unchanged for the zones that
  remain. Extend `COMMON_TIMEZONES` in `main.js` to add more.
* Changed a failed city lookup to say why (e.g. a network error) instead of
  showing the same "No matches." as a genuinely unknown place.

## 0.3.1

* Reverted the resolution scaling introduced and then withdrawn during
  0.3.0 — it broke the layout. Sizes are back to their fixed values, with
  the Settings → Appearance font-size slider as the only scaling control.
  To be revisited.

## 0.3.0

* Added a **Location** tab in Settings: a searchable time zone picker
  covering all ~418 IANA zones, 12/24-hour clock format, and a
  seconds toggle. Daylight saving is applied automatically — the zone
  database and its DST rules come from `Intl`, so there's nothing bundled
  to go stale.
* Added a searchable city picker for the weather location (Open-Meteo
  geocoding, no API key), replacing hand-editing latitude/longitude in
  `config.json`. Choosing a city also sets the matching time zone.
* Added a **Weather widget** tab: three layout styles (Detailed, Compact,
  Minimal), Celsius/Fahrenheit, and independent toggles for the weather
  icon, condition text, location name, feels-like temperature, humidity,
  and wind speed. Only the fields actually being shown are requested from
  the API.
* Changed the clock to only tick every second when seconds are actually
  displayed, rather than always.
* Changed weather failures to log to `error.log` instead of being
  swallowed silently.

## 0.2.0

* Renamed the project from CouchTato to CouchUI. This covers the display
  name, the npm package name (`couch-launcher` → `couch-ui`), the project
  folder (`tv-launcher` → `couch-ui`), and the systemd service
  (`couch-launcher.service` → `couch-ui.service`).

**Migrating an existing install.** Electron derives its settings directory
from the package name, so the rename moves it from
`~/.config/couch-launcher/` to `~/.config/couch-ui/`. Your theme,
background, font, update and pinned-playlist settings live there, as does
your Spotify login. Carry them over before first launch:

```bash
mv ~/.config/couch-launcher ~/.config/couch-ui
```

Skip this and nothing breaks — you'll just start from defaults and need to
sign into Spotify again.

If you had autostart enabled, retire the old service too:

```bash
systemctl --user disable --now couch-launcher.service
rm -f ~/.config/systemd/user/couch-launcher.service
./install.sh   # re-registers it as couch-ui.service
```

## 0.1.1

* Fixed music not playing unless Spotify was already open before starting
  the launcher. The Web API can only control a player that already exists,
  so from a cold start there was nothing to play on. CouchUI now starts
  the Spotify desktop client itself on the first play (or first press of Y)
  and waits for it to register as a Connect device before playing.
* Added `spotify.launchCommand` to `config.json` for setups where the
  desktop client installs under a different command name.
* Changed the "no device found" error into specific, actionable messages —
  distinguishing "Spotify isn't installed / wrong command name" from
  "Spotify started but was too slow to come online".

## 0.1.0

* Added adaptive text color for image backgrounds: the background image is
  sampled (downscaled to 32x32 off-screen, ~1ms, once per image load) and
  clock, weather, and the footer hint legend each get their own text color
  based on what's actually behind them. Runs per image, so slideshows
  re-evaluate on every change.
* Added high-variance detection for background regions — where a region is
  too visually busy for any single text color to work across it, that text
  gets a stronger shadow instead of a color that only half-works.
* Fixed the black/white text threshold, which was set to an
  intuitive-looking 0.4 but is mathematically 0.179 (the luminance where
  black and white give equal contrast). The old value picked white text on
  mid-gray backgrounds at 3.95:1 where black would have given 5.32:1. This
  also affected solid-color backgrounds, not just images.
* Added this version log.

## 0.0.9

* Reverted the packaging work (AppImage/.deb/.rpm builds, electron-builder,
  desktop entry) — back to running from source with `npm start`.
* Reverted `config.json` to living in the app folder. It had been moved to
  the userData folder only because a system-installed app can't write to
  its own install directory; with packaging gone, the indirection had no
  purpose.

## 0.0.8

* Added a Settings → Updates tab: current version, last-checked time, a
  "Search for update" button, and an automatic-updates toggle backed by a
  24h scheduler that survives restarts.
* Added `updater.js` as a deliberate stub — the surrounding wiring is
  complete and calls it, so filling in the real check is a one-file change.
  See DOCUMENTATION.md §3.8.

## 0.0.7

* Added a "Recently Played Songs" row to the Spotify home screen —
  individual tracks, as distinct from "Jump back in", which groups by the
  playlist/album a track was played from.
* Added playlist pinning: pinned playlists sort to the front of "Your
  Playlists" and are marked. Bound to X on the controller and the X key.
* Fixed Spotify tiles stretching to absurd heights on long playlist names —
  titles now clamp to two lines, subtitles to one, and the text panel has a
  fixed height so tiles in a row stay uniform.
* Fixed text and icons (including the settings cog and power button) going
  unreadable in Light mode with a solid-color background. The auto-contrast
  color was being applied globally, including to elements sitting on their
  own opaque panel where it never belonged. Split into two independent
  variable sets: one for panel text, one for text sitting directly on the
  background.
* Removed the track count from playlist tiles.

## 0.0.6

* Moved the Now Playing bar above the footer hint legend.
* Added a progress bar to the Now Playing bar, above the track name.
* Changed the play/pause button to reflect actual state — a pause icon when
  playing, a play icon when paused, instead of a static combined icon.

## 0.0.5

* Added accent icons to the main tiles (browser, Spotify, controller, movie
  clapperboard, folder), colored per-tile and legible in both themes.
* Added tile icon support to `config.json` via an `icon` field.
* Fixed the Kodi tile not launching — the kodi-omega snap installs its
  binary as `kodi-omega`, not `kodi`.
* Fixed `vaInitialize failed` warnings on startup in VMs. Disabling hardware
  acceleration alone didn't stop it; VAAPI decode/encode is a separate
  Chromium feature that probes independently and had to be disabled too.

## 0.0.4

* Added custom backgrounds: solid color (preset palette), single image, or
  a slideshow with configurable interval and crossfade duration.
* Added font customization — four bundled OFL-licensed families (Roboto,
  Open Sans, Merriweather, JetBrains Mono), custom font upload, plus size,
  color, and opacity. Defaults are unchanged unless explicitly set.
* Added submenu support: a tile with `"type": "submenu"` opens a nested
  grid, supporting arbitrary nesting for grouping apps.
* Fixed Light mode washing out custom backgrounds — the scrim was near-opaque
  and theme-colored; it's now a light legibility aid that leaves the
  background visible.
* Fixed long CPU names clipping in the Settings window (a flexbox
  `min-width: 0` overflow issue).
* Fixed accent colors shifting slightly between Dark and Light themes; they
  now share one definition. Light mode's text color was also darkened from a
  washed-out charcoal toward true black.

## 0.0.3

* Added a Spotify home screen with horizontally scrolling rows: search,
  Jump back in, Your Playlists, and mood/genre rows.
* Added Spotify search with an on-screen controller-navigable keyboard;
  physical keyboards can type directly.
* Added a floating overlay scrollbar for Spotify rows, replacing the
  permanent native scrollbar.
* Changed search result order to Tracks, Albums, then Playlists.

## 0.0.2

* Changed all on-screen error reporting to write to `error.log` instead —
  errors previously appeared as toasts or raw command output dumped over the
  tile grid.
* Added catch-all handlers so uncaught exceptions and rejected promises are
  logged rather than lost.
* Fixed Restart and Shutdown doing nothing from the power menu. Two stacked
  bugs: the command was spawned detached and reported success without
  waiting, and the caller never checked the result — so failures were
  invisible at both layers.

## 0.0.1

* Changed the Spotify Now Playing bar from a permanent fixture to a floating
  overlay that appears while playing and fades out after pausing.
* Replaced the local-file/VLC browsing hub with a plain Kodi launch tile —
  Kodi already provides a full library UI (posters, resume, watched state),
  so there was nothing worth rebuilding around it.
